[{:db/id 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-80d2fda3-c051-5035-ae22-72b7e72df2b4" "question-1ada40db-1757-59fe-9612-8be9b0df34b6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179496 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n- the part is the number of kilometers Winston drove the first day, which is $110,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of kilometers he drove, which is $200.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 110 \\div 200 = \\dfrac{110}{200}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{110}{200} = \\dfrac{110 \\div 2}{200 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{55}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{55}{100} = 55 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Winston drove $55 \\%$ of the total distance on the first day." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179820 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e7deab4b-7eef-5f1d-8c28-150c55d5122c" "question-fb35eeb2-cf93-5ec3-9596-7f82799783b3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179497 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of home runs, which is $9,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of extra-base hits Matt connected, which is $75.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 9 \\div 75 = \\dfrac{9}{75}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{75}=\\dfrac{9 \\div 3}{75 \\div 3}=\\dfrac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{25} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 4}{25 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{12}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{100} = 12 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12 \\%$ of extra-base hits were home runs." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-80d2fda3-c051-5035-ae22-72b7e72df2b4" :question/id #uuid "80d2fda3-c051-5035-ae22-72b7e72df2b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-147021" :question/problem "Ashley is watching a TV series with $50$ episodes. So far, she has watched $8$ episodes. What percentage of the TV series has Ashley watched?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of episodes Ashley has watched, which is $8,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of episodes, which is $50.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=8÷50=\\frac{8}{50}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{50}=\\frac{8\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{16}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{100}=16%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ashley has watched $16%$ of the TV series." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cb486c6b-701a-5219-b492-47c6cfbdb546"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb486c6b-701a-5219-b492-47c6cfbdb546" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb486c6b-701a-5219-b492-47c6cfbdb546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-afaae9fc-572f-5d6a-a0fb-c3328f842a37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ec247bd1-9d21-5cb2-8c16-c45e83cff7fa" "answer-afaae9fc-572f-5d6a-a0fb-c3328f842a37" "answer-3732cf4b-7032-5a08-a2ae-523eea2936a5" "answer-1b5741df-80a6-5873-8f04-d0e69effa41a" "answer-e59c5448-f727-5f75-b9c4-19669e8914c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec247bd1-9d21-5cb2-8c16-c45e83cff7fa" :answer/id #uuid "ec247bd1-9d21-5cb2-8c16-c45e83cff7fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afaae9fc-572f-5d6a-a0fb-c3328f842a37" :answer/id #uuid "afaae9fc-572f-5d6a-a0fb-c3328f842a37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3732cf4b-7032-5a08-a2ae-523eea2936a5" :answer/id #uuid "3732cf4b-7032-5a08-a2ae-523eea2936a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b5741df-80a6-5873-8f04-d0e69effa41a" :answer/id #uuid "1b5741df-80a6-5873-8f04-d0e69effa41a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e59c5448-f727-5f75-b9c4-19669e8914c5" :answer/id #uuid "e59c5448-f727-5f75-b9c4-19669e8914c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ada40db-1757-59fe-9612-8be9b0df34b6" :question/id #uuid "1ada40db-1757-59fe-9612-8be9b0df34b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93122" :question/problem "David saved $$500$ to buy a new cellphone. If he spent only $$420$ to buy the cellphone, what percentage of David's savings did he spend on the phone?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the money David spent, which is $420,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total amount of money saved, which is $500.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=420÷500=\\frac{42}{50}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{42}{50}=\\frac{42\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{84}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{84}{100}=84%\n$$\n\nTherefore, David spent $84%$ of the total money saved." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-579fcabb-9f51-5ad2-89db-28010c6fd50f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-579fcabb-9f51-5ad2-89db-28010c6fd50f" :answer-field/id #uuid "579fcabb-9f51-5ad2-89db-28010c6fd50f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-92cf3ebe-9ba5-5ef6-b89a-7e9b43c79e11" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72ed0da1-b2bd-5495-9858-33ebc96fdf26" "answer-33e02cd8-76ea-56bf-be11-5f0c1eaf821a" "answer-92cf3ebe-9ba5-5ef6-b89a-7e9b43c79e11" "answer-6fca0c7f-964b-5c6f-9bdd-5cc809a36efc" "answer-6b61940c-b90b-5e0d-bf86-e66846adfd2b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72ed0da1-b2bd-5495-9858-33ebc96fdf26" :answer/id #uuid "72ed0da1-b2bd-5495-9858-33ebc96fdf26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "76%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33e02cd8-76ea-56bf-be11-5f0c1eaf821a" :answer/id #uuid "33e02cd8-76ea-56bf-be11-5f0c1eaf821a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92cf3ebe-9ba5-5ef6-b89a-7e9b43c79e11" :answer/id #uuid "92cf3ebe-9ba5-5ef6-b89a-7e9b43c79e11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "84%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fca0c7f-964b-5c6f-9bdd-5cc809a36efc" :answer/id #uuid "6fca0c7f-964b-5c6f-9bdd-5cc809a36efc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "86%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b61940c-b90b-5e0d-bf86-e66846adfd2b" :answer/id #uuid "6b61940c-b90b-5e0d-bf86-e66846adfd2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "88%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180240 :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of unripe watermelons, which is $16,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of watermelons, which is $200.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=16÷200=\\frac{16}{200}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{200}=\\frac{16÷2}{200÷2}=\\frac{8}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{100}=8%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $8%$ of the watermelons were unripe." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186178984 :answer-field/correct 17592186178989 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7deab4b-7eef-5f1d-8c28-150c55d5122c" :question/id #uuid "e7deab4b-7eef-5f1d-8c28-150c55d5122c" :question/math-academy-id "q-147035" :question/problem "There are $52$ cards and $13$ clubs in a poker deck. What percentage of the deck are clubs?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of clubs in the deck, which is $13,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of cards in the deck, which is $52.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=13÷52=\\frac{13}{52}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{52}=\\frac{13÷13}{52÷13}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}=25%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25%$ of the poker deck are clubs." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5e2d972-7257-5252-93de-1eebd94a58c7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5e2d972-7257-5252-93de-1eebd94a58c7" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e2d972-7257-5252-93de-1eebd94a58c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ba5d8b56-1d26-5a84-8290-232659c48665" :answer-field/choices ["answer-205b8b00-0213-5e30-b415-2055e4c4dd02" "answer-ba5d8b56-1d26-5a84-8290-232659c48665" "answer-4d83be22-ce90-53a2-8fc7-684eb63b38c6" "answer-2aaaf21f-eb95-57c0-af53-78ab32712771" "answer-ffedf323-2f2f-5902-be03-b70e53b27538"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-205b8b00-0213-5e30-b415-2055e4c4dd02" :answer/id #uuid "205b8b00-0213-5e30-b415-2055e4c4dd02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba5d8b56-1d26-5a84-8290-232659c48665" :answer/id #uuid "ba5d8b56-1d26-5a84-8290-232659c48665" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d83be22-ce90-53a2-8fc7-684eb63b38c6" :answer/id #uuid "4d83be22-ce90-53a2-8fc7-684eb63b38c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2aaaf21f-eb95-57c0-af53-78ab32712771" :answer/id #uuid "2aaaf21f-eb95-57c0-af53-78ab32712771" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffedf323-2f2f-5902-be03-b70e53b27538" :answer/id #uuid "ffedf323-2f2f-5902-be03-b70e53b27538" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fb35eeb2-cf93-5ec3-9596-7f82799783b3" :question/id #uuid "fb35eeb2-cf93-5ec3-9596-7f82799783b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-93128" :question/problem "On average, George exercises for $120$ minutes each day. Of this time, he spends $54$ minutes running. What percentage of his exercise time does George spend running?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of minutes George runs, which is $54,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of minutes George exercises, which is $120.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=54÷120=\\frac{54}{120}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{54}{120}=\\frac{54÷6}{120÷6}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=45%\n$$\n\nTherefore, George runs for $45%$ of his overall training time." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77a90351-e491-5706-b578-ae7f10a99eed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77a90351-e491-5706-b578-ae7f10a99eed" :answer-field/id #uuid "77a90351-e491-5706-b578-ae7f10a99eed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c980d8c-7cd5-5911-892f-f92121a7f872" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b94bb37f-f961-5fa0-99ef-3d1732458f10" "answer-6c980d8c-7cd5-5911-892f-f92121a7f872" "answer-258b396e-193a-5152-ab71-65790f071bcc" "answer-e9c0a062-fc38-5837-af40-23d1b71cba19" "answer-67786787-3341-5686-8d81-9fccf9af01a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b94bb37f-f961-5fa0-99ef-3d1732458f10" :answer/id #uuid "b94bb37f-f961-5fa0-99ef-3d1732458f10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c980d8c-7cd5-5911-892f-f92121a7f872" :answer/id #uuid "6c980d8c-7cd5-5911-892f-f92121a7f872" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-258b396e-193a-5152-ab71-65790f071bcc" :answer/id #uuid "258b396e-193a-5152-ab71-65790f071bcc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9c0a062-fc38-5837-af40-23d1b71cba19" :answer/id #uuid "e9c0a062-fc38-5837-af40-23d1b71cba19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "55%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67786787-3341-5686-8d81-9fccf9af01a0" :answer/id #uuid "67786787-3341-5686-8d81-9fccf9af01a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180661 :tutorial/content "If we start with the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} ,\n$$\n\nwe can use the relationship between multiplication and division to write down a formula to calculate a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how to apply this formula using a real-world example.\n\nSuppose that $54$ out of $300$ boxes of grapes in a supermarket contain red grapes. What percentage of the boxes contain red grapes?\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of red grapes, which is $54$, and the whole is the total number of grapes, which is $300.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 54 \\div 300 = \\dfrac{54}{300}.\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{54}{300} = \\dfrac{54 \\div 3}{300 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{18}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{100} = 18 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\%$ of the boxes contain red grapes." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179897 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-48fe30a0-5320-52ad-9ed5-45000abef204"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-48fe30a0-5320-52ad-9ed5-45000abef204" :question/id #uuid "48fe30a0-5320-52ad-9ed5-45000abef204" :question/math-academy-id "q-126532" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/04/0470588a034222b183694b087d417e8ead0d2fd72357e796373794d00523708f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7bb86058-0e7f-5528-b79d-a77b82e246b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7bb86058-0e7f-5528-b79d-a77b82e246b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb86058-0e7f-5528-b79d-a77b82e246b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f21bd52-4aa7-5ea8-a034-d3560d1d5b01" "answer-867e3063-71a8-5b3f-ba08-0e4184738773" "answer-6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" "answer-76be6580-ecd9-54fd-be34-2e6d6eb3bf26" "answer-648aec14-e325-59f4-a6ad-4fe7fed0f4d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f21bd52-4aa7-5ea8-a034-d3560d1d5b01" :answer/id #uuid "1f21bd52-4aa7-5ea8-a034-d3560d1d5b01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-867e3063-71a8-5b3f-ba08-0e4184738773" :answer/id #uuid "867e3063-71a8-5b3f-ba08-0e4184738773" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer/id #uuid "6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76be6580-ecd9-54fd-be34-2e6d6eb3bf26" :answer/id #uuid "76be6580-ecd9-54fd-be34-2e6d6eb3bf26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-648aec14-e325-59f4-a6ad-4fe7fed0f4d4" :answer/id #uuid "648aec14-e325-59f4-a6ad-4fe7fed0f4d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-10fd1bae-a694-5bc5-b3cf-fcf342c64799" "question-a150df94-0dd2-516e-ab2f-283c22ad4f30"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-10fd1bae-a694-5bc5-b3cf-fcf342c64799" :question/id #uuid "10fd1bae-a694-5bc5-b3cf-fcf342c64799" :question/math-academy-id "q-126677" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb2da6fbb22b19d027212685ca521f5186a831502617c2089766c5e9fe739696.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x))\\,dx+({x}^{2}+3xy)\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/48/4822bfa926feccb0a12c14580ac325f4f10a0e4f815d0247b2231bf08b8c2326.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x))\\,dx+({x}^{2}+3xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3be5c97-a9dc-5eec-ad89-fde98181ed37"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3be5c97-a9dc-5eec-ad89-fde98181ed37" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3be5c97-a9dc-5eec-ad89-fde98181ed37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7378996c-e490-54ee-8ec3-96c3c35dfd15" :answer-field/choices ["answer-21c3618c-e307-593c-be1d-ae62e518df4f" "answer-7378996c-e490-54ee-8ec3-96c3c35dfd15" "answer-1e66697d-f4a8-5190-9c92-04e431212fa0" "answer-5f8d4fbc-5dde-5631-9343-463c2c790561" "answer-876615df-473a-5e4a-9661-1a6b746a5404"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21c3618c-e307-593c-be1d-ae62e518df4f" :answer/id #uuid "21c3618c-e307-593c-be1d-ae62e518df4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7378996c-e490-54ee-8ec3-96c3c35dfd15" :answer/id #uuid "7378996c-e490-54ee-8ec3-96c3c35dfd15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e66697d-f4a8-5190-9c92-04e431212fa0" :answer/id #uuid "1e66697d-f4a8-5190-9c92-04e431212fa0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f8d4fbc-5dde-5631-9343-463c2c790561" :answer/id #uuid "5f8d4fbc-5dde-5631-9343-463c2c790561" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-876615df-473a-5e4a-9661-1a6b746a5404" :answer/id #uuid "876615df-473a-5e4a-9661-1a6b746a5404" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a150df94-0dd2-516e-ab2f-283c22ad4f30" :question/id #uuid "a150df94-0dd2-516e-ab2f-283c22ad4f30" :question/math-academy-id "q-126707" :question/problem "![](images/18/184b50ce54b01453d5b645eef7edc186823e8ae34afaefa2527e34122c1722cc.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/93/93a1c20377ddd0731c6190712d9d6d77551124fa33001334e84f52049bb6b800.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1+2{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(1+2{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 9,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-294a80e3-eaa7-5f33-b4a3-69d8d31c5ae7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-294a80e3-eaa7-5f33-b4a3-69d8d31c5ae7" :answer-field/id #uuid "294a80e3-eaa7-5f33-b4a3-69d8d31c5ae7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4d3491ea-d8ce-5605-97f6-fd42cbc08809" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14eb9fcd-7abf-50ea-acb9-8e896cb286b9" "answer-4752d255-6d05-5b8c-9bc7-d3d564d74fbb" "answer-778250fc-a408-537b-8277-d49912ff9021" "answer-4d3491ea-d8ce-5605-97f6-fd42cbc08809" "answer-62d8034f-3c24-592c-b26b-73464d3851ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14eb9fcd-7abf-50ea-acb9-8e896cb286b9" :answer/id #uuid "14eb9fcd-7abf-50ea-acb9-8e896cb286b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4752d255-6d05-5b8c-9bc7-d3d564d74fbb" :answer/id #uuid "4752d255-6d05-5b8c-9bc7-d3d564d74fbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-778250fc-a408-537b-8277-d49912ff9021" :answer/id #uuid "778250fc-a408-537b-8277-d49912ff9021" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d3491ea-d8ce-5605-97f6-fd42cbc08809" :answer/id #uuid "4d3491ea-d8ce-5605-97f6-fd42cbc08809" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62d8034f-3c24-592c-b26b-73464d3851ba" :answer/id #uuid "62d8034f-3c24-592c-b26b-73464d3851ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{9}({r}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3227ab6b-7873-5e0c-8df6-f96bbac69dd6" "question-a7d3359f-1209-53f1-b438-cca94d3b404c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3227ab6b-7873-5e0c-8df6-f96bbac69dd6" :question/id #uuid "3227ab6b-7873-5e0c-8df6-f96bbac69dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126735" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f56006dd06fab3bbc736c45ac6e9d65a401b88e0a9eb8a5db404fb67b89977e.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/f3/f393d0facc98fe365e2c622e24223d6045b48b490937ba6e2f25d86d7eb4810f.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-8{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{2}-(-8{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[2{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}32\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16ca1c58-b325-53c7-b363-8ad3f071412f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16ca1c58-b325-53c7-b363-8ad3f071412f" :answer-field/id #uuid "16ca1c58-b325-53c7-b363-8ad3f071412f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9f77bfdf-543d-55d9-98f8-52a2f2343148" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9f77bfdf-543d-55d9-98f8-52a2f2343148" "answer-e782fbf2-3eee-5d13-a745-b3e38c6a490e" "answer-a61402b4-43f9-5d6a-b293-8db1d57747f2" "answer-76e9497e-95ac-551f-b7f9-a976c2b420b5" "answer-8daee35f-46c8-5466-b3b4-c225766c4214"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f77bfdf-543d-55d9-98f8-52a2f2343148" :answer/id #uuid "9f77bfdf-543d-55d9-98f8-52a2f2343148" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e782fbf2-3eee-5d13-a745-b3e38c6a490e" :answer/id #uuid "e782fbf2-3eee-5d13-a745-b3e38c6a490e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a61402b4-43f9-5d6a-b293-8db1d57747f2" :answer/id #uuid "a61402b4-43f9-5d6a-b293-8db1d57747f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76e9497e-95ac-551f-b7f9-a976c2b420b5" :answer/id #uuid "76e9497e-95ac-551f-b7f9-a976c2b420b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8daee35f-46c8-5466-b3b4-c225766c4214" :answer/id #uuid "8daee35f-46c8-5466-b3b4-c225766c4214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a7d3359f-1209-53f1-b438-cca94d3b404c" :question/id #uuid "a7d3359f-1209-53f1-b438-cca94d3b404c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126737" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb6a49b2172b3a60676ef74a244298eff05ffc65be6feca089c4a953757f2e59.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d816d9153e22dd109e20e3e387f6a0028e3f6a9eaf1d284dfae8b5fdf54cfc6e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3e4c3bc-9dab-52ff-8019-5399ade463a4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3e4c3bc-9dab-52ff-8019-5399ade463a4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3e4c3bc-9dab-52ff-8019-5399ade463a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bbe1ae9b-b60a-5838-a56d-dad4b77ce8e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24f7045b-5228-59f7-a899-69db8a29caed" "answer-8f089fd7-57f0-5309-8b86-329b4b579d58" "answer-64eeeb99-1d14-5f8d-8274-71cc4b59f7db" "answer-bbe1ae9b-b60a-5838-a56d-dad4b77ce8e6" "answer-b143a56b-f222-5495-aa7a-9092c0670a7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24f7045b-5228-59f7-a899-69db8a29caed" :answer/id #uuid "24f7045b-5228-59f7-a899-69db8a29caed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f089fd7-57f0-5309-8b86-329b4b579d58" :answer/id #uuid "8f089fd7-57f0-5309-8b86-329b4b579d58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64eeeb99-1d14-5f8d-8274-71cc4b59f7db" :answer/id #uuid "64eeeb99-1d14-5f8d-8274-71cc4b59f7db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbe1ae9b-b60a-5838-a56d-dad4b77ce8e6" :answer/id #uuid "bbe1ae9b-b60a-5838-a56d-dad4b77ce8e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b143a56b-f222-5495-aa7a-9092c0670a7e" :answer/id #uuid "b143a56b-f222-5495-aa7a-9092c0670a7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9407eb8b-2b41-54d6-91e3-4f75b70fbcd7" :activity/id #uuid "9407eb8b-2b41-54d6-91e3-4f75b70fbcd7" :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050332 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-d286d5c7-a12c-594f-9ccb-48543ab9d3ae" "step-33aa2c01-ef8c-530d-9a87-558d09200d7c" "step-d88b6d01-4f98-5073-ad53-8b68c9498d2f" "step-b39e3053-19ff-54d9-8a03-cabe47ece267" "step-cb7edd78-bd6a-5d85-baab-eeb17426674d"] :activity/first-step "step-d286d5c7-a12c-594f-9ccb-48543ab9d3ae" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d286d5c7-a12c-594f-9ccb-48543ab9d3ae" :step/id #uuid "d286d5c7-a12c-594f-9ccb-48543ab9d3ae" :step/content "question-48fe30a0-5320-52ad-9ed5-45000abef204" :step/next "step-33aa2c01-ef8c-530d-9a87-558d09200d7c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33aa2c01-ef8c-530d-9a87-558d09200d7c" :step/id #uuid "33aa2c01-ef8c-530d-9a87-558d09200d7c" :step/content "question-10fd1bae-a694-5bc5-b3cf-fcf342c64799" :step/next "step-d88b6d01-4f98-5073-ad53-8b68c9498d2f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d88b6d01-4f98-5073-ad53-8b68c9498d2f" :step/id #uuid "d88b6d01-4f98-5073-ad53-8b68c9498d2f" :step/content "question-a150df94-0dd2-516e-ab2f-283c22ad4f30" :step/next "step-b39e3053-19ff-54d9-8a03-cabe47ece267" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b39e3053-19ff-54d9-8a03-cabe47ece267" :step/id #uuid "b39e3053-19ff-54d9-8a03-cabe47ece267" :step/content "question-3227ab6b-7873-5e0c-8df6-f96bbac69dd6" :step/next "step-cb7edd78-bd6a-5d85-baab-eeb17426674d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cb7edd78-bd6a-5d85-baab-eeb17426674d" :step/id #uuid "cb7edd78-bd6a-5d85-baab-eeb17426674d" :step/content "question-a7d3359f-1209-53f1-b438-cca94d3b404c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186271589 :answer-field/choices 17592186271222] [:db/retract 17592186125118 :question/answer-fields 17592186123308] {:db/id 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f070189a-9c50-508c-9a92-9de0a01ed5ec" "question-a9d1798c-418f-589b-8399-947e298c1f4a" "question-6acf0a48-db0b-58e8-8817-08a155161bed"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124436 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/29/29402a77d81267af571672df82b86e1d9853f17f26b6ad0a139a0d0f4ea906c7.svg) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/dd/dd3981fa517de2829a94c8b50c09e66a9f9830a3ddcf2651b583fb4da348c93c.svg) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6afa206b-a720-5d99-9269-3c50ce4125f7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124330 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is invertible? \n\nI. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\nII. $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=0$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/3e/3eba90e8e26e611656e00139533ea0e91628b7d53316fe0fbb33961d75211d04.svg) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4841bd4c-e53a-5f1a-ae44-1f39564910ef"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124435 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ then $\\textrm{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\nII. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})\\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{T}$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/d5/d54f9467c22c22ade32bbb49f611c545140b9ce5d11610c04dd7f946d83ac789.svg) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3,$ the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272284 :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}}={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}}=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom the missing expression are $\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f070189a-9c50-508c-9a92-9de0a01ed5ec" :question/id #uuid "f070189a-9c50-508c-9a92-9de0a01ed5ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-141456" :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expression are $\\{0\\}$ and $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-712c7f36-648a-59ce-b5e6-2d0dcc22e8d2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-712c7f36-648a-59ce-b5e6-2d0dcc22e8d2" :answer-field/id #uuid "712c7f36-648a-59ce-b5e6-2d0dcc22e8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4c867958-e95e-5917-a788-9cd854240b91" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d66b85e5-4ade-5bb2-8d27-85b829c3c7d2" "answer-972660c3-34f0-56ad-abc9-d0eb75375474" "answer-4c867958-e95e-5917-a788-9cd854240b91" "answer-0ad91a4a-06f2-5d9a-8655-5263c848d3f2" "answer-0e8919b8-5643-5b79-8930-830170d078aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d66b85e5-4ade-5bb2-8d27-85b829c3c7d2" :answer/id #uuid "d66b85e5-4ade-5bb2-8d27-85b829c3c7d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ and $0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-972660c3-34f0-56ad-abc9-d0eb75375474" :answer/id #uuid "972660c3-34f0-56ad-abc9-d0eb75375474" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R∖\\{0\\}$ and $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c867958-e95e-5917-a788-9cd854240b91" :answer/id